<speak>
<p>We will evaluate the residue of the full alternating sum, script S. Let u be odd and greater than one, and let I contain its distinct prime divisors. For each subset S, zeta sub S is the product of its chosen prime roots. The empty subset gives one. The sum uses sign minus one to the size of S, and the normalized value E at zeta sub S divided by c at zeta sub S. Work in the same ring A, with i, those roots, and one over u adjoined to the integers.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Node five gives a coefficientwise congruence: E of P is congruent to the all-ones polynomial J of P modulo one plus i. J contains every power from zero through u squared minus one. Evaluation preserves that congruence. At every subset input, E and J have the same residue. We want to replace every numerator while preserving its sign and denominator.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>The denominator step is justified by units in A. The inverse of c at zeta sub S is its complex conjugate divided by u squared. Multiplying c by that inverse gives one exactly. Reduce this identity: the two images still multiply to one. Thus the denominator stays invertible after reduction. Multiply the congruence between E and J by this inverse. The normalized values have the same residue. This uses a unit of the evaluation ring, not merely a nonzero complex value. Make the replacement in every term of script S.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Node eight point one gives an exact zero: J at zeta sub S equals zero whenever S is nonempty. Each corresponding whole term is zero, since its denominator is a unit. Remove all those terms. Only the empty subset remains. Its sign is positive one and its input is one. Therefore script S is congruent to J of one divided by c of one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Node eight point two evaluates this last quotient. J of one equals u squared, and c of one equals positive or negative u. Their quotient is positive or negative u. Because u is odd, both signs have residue one modulo one plus i. The full alternating sum therefore has residue one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>See the same calculation for u equal to fifteen. The four subsets of three and five have signs plus, minus, minus, plus. Their input orders are one, three, five, and fifteen. The three nonempty-subset numerators vanish exactly. The surviving quotient is two hundred twenty-five divided by positive or negative fifteen. It equals positive or negative fifteen, with residue one.<break time="600ms"/>The number of distinct prime divisors can change, but exactly one term survives. Keep this residue one beside Node seven's residue zero for the very same sum in the very same quotient ring. The next step compares them.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
